@meta
id: 16-13
title: Close the window, not the editor
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: window-nav, split-window
par: 90
minutes: 6
vscode: close the split, then close the other split, then find the file you started in
open: order.provider.ts

@brief
Windows open cheaply, so they have to close cheaply too, and the
important thing to know is what `:q` means once there is more than one.
It closes the current window. Vim stays open with the rest. Only when
the last window goes does vim exit. That is the same command you have
been using to leave since 02-01, with the same meaning: "I am done with
this view."

`Ctrl-W c` closes the current window without writing anything. `Ctrl-W
q` is `:q` as a chord. And the one you will use most: `Ctrl-W o`, or
`:only`, closes every window except the current one. Two reference
files open beside the code, finished looking, one chord, back to the
code alone.

The provider has two typos that the two reference files settle. `ship`
compares the status to `'PENDNG'`, and `order.entity.ts` says the
value is `'PENDING'`. It also throws `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPED`, and
`errors.json` says the code is `ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED`. Open both
references in windows, fix the provider, and leave. Neither reference
changes.

@keys
| `:q` | close this window; vim exits only when it was the last one |
| `Ctrl-W c` | close this window |
| `Ctrl-W q` | `:q` as a chord |
| `Ctrl-W o` `:only` | close every other window |
| `:qa` | close every window and exit, refusing if anything is unsaved |

@hints
`:sp errors.json` then `:vs order.entity.ts` gives three windows: two
references on top, the provider below. `Ctrl-W j` gets you back down to
it.
--
`Ctrl-W o` from the provider window closes the other two. Now the fixes:
`/PENDNG`, `cw`, `PENDING`; `/SHIPED`, `cw`, `SHIPPED`.
--
Single window, single file: `:wq` is enough. Had you left the
references open, `:wq` would have closed only the provider's window
and vim would still be running; `:wqa` or `:qa` is the reflex for
that.

@solution
    :sp errors.json<Enter>  :vs order.entity.ts<Enter>  Ctrl-W j  Ctrl-W o
    /PENDNG<Enter>  cw PENDING <Esc>  /SHIPED<Enter>  cw SHIPPED <Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Eighty keystrokes, of which the two references cost forty and closing
them cost two.

`:q` closing a window rather than vim is the thing that surprises
people the first time. It is also the reason `:qa` exists. A tidy exit
from a many-window session is `:wqa` if everything should be written,
`:qa` if you want vim to tell you what is not, and `:qa!` if you mean
it. `Ctrl-W o` before any of those is a good habit: one window, one
buffer, and you can see exactly what `:wq` is about to do.

A window closing does not remove anything from the buffer list. The
entity and the errors file are still there for `:b` after `Ctrl-W o`,
which is often what you want: out of sight, one fragment away.

@start order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDNG') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { injectable, inject } from 'tsyringe';
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';
import { PaymentService } from '../../infrastructure/services/payment.service';

@injectable()
export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(
    @inject(OrderRepository) protected orderRepository: OrderRepository,
    @inject(PaymentService) protected paymentService: PaymentService,
  ) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const order = await this.orderRepository.findOne({ where: { id } });

    if (!order) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND');
    }

    return order;
  }

  public async ship(id: string) {
    const order = await this.findOne(id);

    if (order.status !== 'PENDING') {
      throw exception('ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED');
    }

    return this.orderRepository.update({
      where: { id },
      values: { status: 'SHIPPED' },
    });
  }
}

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@start errors.json
{
  "ORDER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "ORDER_ALREADY_SHIPPED": { "statusCode": 409, "sentry": false },
  "CUSTOMER_NOT_FOUND": { "statusCode": 404, "sentry": false },
  "REFUND_FAILED": { "statusCode": 502, "sentry": true }
}
